{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'expressions\\\\(|def _source|S4|rests_on|def publish_decision' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py; rg -n '35 billion to|195 billion to|ambiguous|e5376|e5081' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/{calc_check.py,small_cases.py,SMALL-DESIGN.md,DESIGN.md} poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/{E,S}/user.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '255,350p'; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11236,11258p'\"","aggregated_output":"   255\t**Part B, blind writers ($0 at the provider).**\n   256\t- **The writers.** Each of the six cases is written once by a fresh Claude subagent and once by a fresh read-only Codex session, 12\n   257\t  sessions in all. Each gets only that case's exact outgoing system text, user text and schema.\n   258\t- **The first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `check` unedited.\n   259\t- **The readers.** Two readers who wrote neither the design nor the answers (a Claude subagent and a Codex session) grade **every**\n   260\t  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\n   261\t  - for each refusal, whether the exact request states the condition (the sentence quoted) and whether the answer was right;\n   262\t  - for each accepted answer, whether each forecast is supported, and whether its recorded conversions or estimates decide its band.\n   263\t- **A third reader** settles disagreements.\n   264\t- **Usable** means accepted, publishable by `publish_decision`, and supported by the readers. For the joint case, both forecasts must\n   265\t  be supported in one reply.\n   266\t\n   267\t**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\n   268\t- an A1 form refused;\n   269\t- an A2 defect not refused by its rule;\n   270\t- an A3 mistake neither impossible nor stated;\n   271\t- a Part B refusal whose condition the request does not state, or that refuses a right answer;\n   272\t- an INCOMPLETE run;\n   273\t- a case with zero usable first replies across both writers;\n   274\t- the joint case without both supported forecasts in one reply.\n   275\t\n   276\t**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n   277\tD725's lines. Only that run measures whether luna can do it.\n 11236\t## D737 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: shrink judging-v13's format to the checks that stop the named defects, then a $0 blind test\n 11237\t\n 11238\t- **The question** (after D736): \"判数字新版本的修补连续三轮审查都在冒新规则（填写说明将从 1 万字涨到约 2 万字，每个答案约 16 个数、每个数要同时满足几十条规则）。Codex 主张修完再审最后一轮；我主张现在改结构。下一步怎么走？\"\n 11239\t  Options:\n 11240\t  - 砍成小格式再盲测 (recommended): keep only the checks that stop five defects (a number misread, wrong arithmetic, a part\n 11241\t    above its whole or added into it, two forecasts of one quarter, an own estimate passed off as the market's); the other rules\n 11242\t    become assumptions shown to readers; council, then the $0 blind test, then a request for the paid re-send;\n 11243\t  - 按原计划再修一轮 (Codex's position);\n 11244\t  - 关掉这条线.\n 11245\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22), the recommended option (\"…成小格式再盲测 (推荐)\").\n 11246\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11247\t  - The judging-v13 format shrinks. Code refuses an answer only for the five defects above and for broken structure (links that do\n 11248\t    not resolve, a cycle, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome not of the question's quantity, a rung that does not hold the\n 11249\t    outcome).\n 11250\t  - Each such rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of DESIGN.md's checks becomes an assumption\n 11251\t    recorded and shown to the readers, never a refusal.\n 11252\t  - The council reviews within that scope. A counterexample that lets none of the five defects through is a reader's matter, not a\n 11253\t    finding.\n 11254\t  - Then the $0 blind test (REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5, with round 51's reader fix). A clean result returns to Alex for the paid re-send under\n 11255\t    D725's lines.\n 11256\t- **Superseded:** REPAIR-DESIGN.md v2's full restatement (D735's finish line), replaced by the small format.\n 11257\t\n 11258\t## D738 · 2026-11-22 · v13s: code refuses a named defect only where the calculation's structure decides it; readers mark the five defects on every reply (council rounds 52–53)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'expressions\\\\(|def _source|S4|rests_on|def publish_decision' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py; rg -n '35 billion to|195 billion to|ambiguous|e5376|e5081' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/{calc_check.py,small_cases.py,SMALL-DESIGN.md,DESIGN.md} poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/{E,S}/user.txt\"","aggregated_output":"2:the five named defects (S1 a number misread, S2 wrong arithmetic, S3/S8 a part above its whole or added into it, S4 two forecasts of one\n80:def expressions(text):\n144:                 'basis_text', 'rests_on')\n340:    \"\"\"The figures beneath fid through inputs and rests_on, at any depth (fid excluded).\"\"\"\n341:    return _upstream_of(fid, figs, lambda f: list(f.get('inputs') or []) + [r for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs])\n345:    \"\"\"[54] The figures of `key` an answer adopts: those reached from its outcome (inputs and rests_on) before any other figure of\n357:        stack.extend(list(f.get('inputs') or []) + [r for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs])\n392:        if kind != 'estimate' and f.get('rests_on'):\n393:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on is for estimates only'))\n414:        for r in f.get('rests_on') or []:\n416:                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on names {r}, which is neither a figure nor a shown passage'))\n426:        hit = any(cyclic(r) for r in list(f.get('inputs') or []) + [r for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs])\n430:        errors.append(('S7', 'a figure depends on itself (through inputs or rests_on)'))\n444:def _sources(passages, figs):\n454:        spans = expressions(text)\n580:    \"\"\"[54] The figures the outcomes adopt: each reached from an outcome (inputs and rests_on) with no figure of its own quantity and\n593:        for r in list(f.get('inputs') or []) + [r for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs]:\n612:            errors.append(('S4', f'{\" | \".join(k)}: {len(ids)} forecasts ({\", \".join(sorted(ids))}); compute it once and use that figure'))\n632:                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\" | \".join(key)} but the calculation has {len(node)} forecast figures for it'))\n636:                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\", \".join(bypass)} for {\" | \".join(key)} beside its forecast figure {node[0]}'))\n724:            rested = {figs[r]['what']['scope'] for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs}\n767:def publish_decision(family, calc, result):\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:31:    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:15:    Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:101:- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:103:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:106:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:109:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:127:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:128:- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:130:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:21:    ('range', re.compile(r'\\$\\s?' + NUM + r'\\s*(?:' + UNIT + r')?\\s*(?:–|-)\\s*\\$?\\s?' + NUM + r'\\s*' + UNIT)),   # \"to\" is ambiguous (\"from $253 billion to $392 billion\")\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:566:                                             'the comparison is ambiguous'))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/{reader-packets,adjudication}/R{06,12}.{claude,codex}.json poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/refusal-review/E-claude.{claude,codex}.json; do printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; nl -ba \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/{reader-packets,adjudication}/R{06,12}.{claude,codex}.json poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/refusal-review/E-claude.{claude,codex}.json; do printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; nl -ba \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R06.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Two reported quarters: FY2027 Q1 $81.62bn (e111) and Q2 $96.22bn (e4962). Q3 is $113.6bn (F6), taken from the outside estimate based on past guidance beats (e5056, with e5054/e5055 and the prior-quarter beat in e3200), against guidance of $108bn (e2652). Q4 is $128.368bn: the writer's own stated assumption of 13% sequential growth (F8), resting on guidance-implied growth of 12.24% (F7) and on e3406, where hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4. The sum is $419.808bn, which falls in rung 2 ($392bn to below $431.2bn). The consensus reference of $411bn (e5204) is also in rung 2. The rung is robust: using guidance as given (Q3 $108bn, Q4 at 12.24% growth, about $121.2bn) totals about $407bn, still rung 2. Rung 1 would need Q4 above about $139.8bn. Rung 3 would need Q4 below about $100.5bn.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F6\", \"F7\"], \"detail\": \"The request names NVIDIA revenue FY2027 Q3 as a shared quantity. Every figure the outcome uses for it must go through one forecast figure, and here that is F6 ($113.6bn). F7 (Q3 sequential growth, 12.24%) instead takes Q3 revenue directly from F4, the $108bn guidance. F8, the Q4 growth estimate, rests on F7, and F9/F10 build on F8. So the outcome's chain uses two different Q3 values: $108bn to set the growth rate, and $113.6bn as the base for Q4 and in the annual sum. The forecast's own Q3 would imply about 18.1% sequential growth, not 12.24%. The effect on the outcome is small (Q4 $128.4bn vs about $122.0bn on the guidance base), and the rung stays 2 either way.\"}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R06.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v4\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"e111 and e4962 give $81.62 billion and $96.22 billion for Q1 and Q2. The answer adopts the $113.6 billion Q3 estimate from e5056, citing the prior guidance beat and Rubin shipments in e3200, e2693, and e5118. It assumes 13% sequential growth in Q4 based on the growth implied by e2652 and the outlook in e3406, tempered by the constraints in e5067, e4771, and e5205. That gives $128.368 billion for Q4 and $419.808 billion for the year, within rung 2.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": [\n    10\t    {\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\"],\n    13\t      \"detail\": \"e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F4 preserves the range in the quoted text but records the midpoint with no qualifier or bounds.\"\n    14\t    },\n    15\t    {\n    16\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    17\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F6\"],\n    18\t      \"detail\": \"The annual forecast uses $113.6 billion as Q3 revenue, while the Q4 growth assumption separately uses $108 billion as Q3 revenue through F7. Both Q3 values feed the annual forecast.\"\n    19\t    }\n    20\t  ]\n    21\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R12.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of 164.25 is F1 (e109, 75.25) plus F2 (e2650, 89.0), which matches e5309's $164bn. The Q3 company revenue estimate F10 of 112.5 sits between guidance F8 (e2652, 108 +/-2%) and the outside estimate F9 (e5056, roughly 113.6). Its stated basis is the prior-quarter beat (e3200), Rubin shipments (e2693), CoreWeave's deployment (e5118) and the supply cap (e5067). The Q4 assumption F12 is 13% sequential growth, the guided Q3 pace F11 (108/96.2-1 = 12.27%) plus a small increase grounded on e3406 and limited by e5067 and e5205; it gives F13 = 127.125. The H2 Data Center share F15 is 93%, slightly above the Q2 share F5 (89/96.2 = 92.5%), grounded on e5061, e5223 and e3406 and consistent with the rising share in e3399. H2 Data Center F16 = 239.625 x 0.93 = 222.85. The FY2027 total F18 = 164.25 + 222.85 = 387.10, which is in rung 2 (at least 350, below 400). Every computed step checks. The market reference F6 = 411 (e5204 consensus) x 0.9252 (reported Q2 share) = 380.24, also in rung 2, and Morningstar's 361 (e2295) falls in the same band.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R12.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v8\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue is $164.25 billion (e109, e2650). The stated assumptions of $112.5 billion in Q3 company revenue, 13% sequential growth in Q4, and a 93% second-half Data Center share yield $222.85 billion in second-half segment revenue. The resulting $387.10 billion falls in rung 2. The assumptions draw on e2652, e3200, e5056, e2693, e5118, e3406, e5061, e5223, e5067, and e5205.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": [\n    10\t    {\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F8\"],\n    13\t      \"detail\": \"e2652 gives $108.0 billion as the guidance midpoint with a plus-or-minus 2% range. F8 quotes that range but records no bounds and sets the qualifier to none.\"\n    14\t    },\n    15\t    {\n    16\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    17\t      \"figures\": [\"F8\", \"F10\", \"F11\", \"F12\", \"F13\"],\n    18\t      \"detail\": \"The Q4 forecast uses $108 billion as Q3 company revenue to calculate the sequential growth benchmark that informs F12, then uses $112.5 billion as Q3 company revenue when applying the chosen growth rate in F13.\"\n    19\t    },\n    20\t    {\n    21\t      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    22\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F5\", \"F6\"],\n    23\t      \"detail\": \"The $411 billion consensus in e5204 covers company revenue. F6 applies the reported Q2 Data Center share to the full year, making the $380.24 billion segment reference depend on the writer's assumption that the Q2 share holds for FY2027.\"\n    24\t    }\n    25\t  ]\n    26\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R06.claude.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"members\": {\n     3\t  \"v4\": {\n     4\t   \"supported\": \"yes\"\n     5\t  }\n     6\t },\n     7\t \"defects\": [\n     8\t  {\n     9\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    10\t   \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    11\t   \"figures\": [\n    12\t    \"F4\",\n    13\t    \"F6\",\n    14\t    \"F7\"\n    15\t   ],\n    16\t   \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    17\t   \"why\": \"F7 = growth(F3, F4) takes Q3 revenue from the $108bn guidance, outside the Q3 forecast F6 ($113.6bn); F7 feeds the outcome through F8 -> F9 -> F10; two Q3 values reach the annual forecast; rung unchanged\"\n    18\t  },\n    19\t  {\n    20\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    21\t   \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    22\t   \"figures\": [\n    23\t    \"F4\"\n    24\t   ],\n    25\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    26\t   \"why\": \"midpoint of a plus-or-minus expression is a permitted form\"\n    27\t  },\n    28\t  {\n    29\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    30\t   \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    31\t   \"figures\": [\n    32\t    \"F4\",\n    33\t    \"F6\"\n    34\t   ],\n    35\t   \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    36\t   \"why\": \"same breach as Reader 1's\"\n    37\t  }\n    38\t ],\n    39\t \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the Claude adjudicator hand-back (decisions and figures verbatim; reasons shortened).\"\n    40\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R06.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v4\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues are $81.62 billion and $96.22 billion (e111, e4962). The $113.6 billion Q3 estimate follows the guidance-beat scenario in e5056, supported by the prior beat and Rubin shipments (e3200, e2693, e5118). The stated 13% Q4 growth assumption starts from the 12.24% growth implied by guidance and assumes a modest lift as Rubin supply builds and hyperscaler growth picks up, with supply and delivery constraints still in place (e3406, e5067, e4771, e5205). This yields $128.368 billion for Q4 and $419.808 billion for FY2027, within rung 2.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F6\", \"F7\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"The request requires 'one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes.' The annual sum and Q4 revenue calculation use F6's $113.6 billion for Q3. F7 uses F4's $108 billion directly to calculate the growth rate that informs F8 and feeds the outcome through F9. That path bypasses F6.\"\n    15\t    },\n    16\t    {\n    17\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    18\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    19\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\"],\n    20\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    21\t      \"why\": \"F4 retains the full expression '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' from e2652. The format rule permits 'low and high or its midpoint as value,' so 108 is valid. The 2% is part of the quoted expression, and the passage supplies no separate numeric qualifier.\"\n    22\t    },\n    23\t    {\n    24\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    25\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    26\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F6\"],\n    27\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    28\t      \"why\": \"The format says to 'Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period ... wherever that quantity is needed.' F4 is allowed as outside support beneath F6. It also supplies a separate $108 billion Q3 input to F7, which informs the Q4 growth assumption, while the annual sum uses F6's $113.6 billion for Q3.\"\n    29\t    }\n    30\t  ]\n    31\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R12.claude.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"members\": {\n     3\t  \"v8\": {\n     4\t   \"supported\": \"yes\"\n     5\t  }\n     6\t },\n     7\t \"defects\": [\n     8\t  {\n     9\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    10\t   \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    11\t   \"figures\": [\n    12\t    \"F8\"\n    13\t   ],\n    14\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    15\t   \"why\": \"midpoint value permitted\"\n    16\t  },\n    17\t  {\n    18\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    19\t   \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    20\t   \"figures\": [\n    21\t    \"F8\",\n    22\t    \"F10\",\n    23\t    \"F11\",\n    24\t    \"F12\",\n    25\t    \"F13\"\n    26\t   ],\n    27\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    28\t   \"why\": \"F10 is the only Q3 value entering the outcome chain; guidance 108 is used only through an outside growth benchmark beneath an estimate\"\n    29\t  },\n    30\t  {\n    31\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    32\t   \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    33\t   \"figures\": [\n    34\t    \"F4\",\n    35\t    \"F5\",\n    36\t    \"F6\"\n    37\t   ],\n    38\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    39\t   \"why\": \"computed from consensus source and reported ratio; no estimate or forecast beneath\"\n    40\t  }\n    41\t ],\n    42\t \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the Claude adjudicator hand-back (decisions and figures verbatim; reasons shortened). Full text in the session record.\"\n    43\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R12.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v8\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"NVIDIA reported $164.25 billion in first-half Data Center revenue (e109, e2650). The prior guidance beat and Rubin shipments support a $112.5 billion Q3 company forecast, below the outside estimate of roughly $113.6 billion given supply constraints (e3200, e2693, e5118, e5056, e5067). The expected pickup in hyperscaler growth and continued Data Center strength support the stated assumptions of 13% sequential growth in Q4 and a 93% second-half Data Center share (e3406, e5223). Those assumptions yield $222.85 billion in second-half Data Center revenue and $387.10 billion for the year, within rung 2.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F8\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"F8 quotes e2652 in full as \\\"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\\" The format permits a plus-or-minus expression to be given as \\\"low and high or its midpoint as value.\\\" The qualifier rule says \\\"none when there is none,\\\" and e2652 has no qualifying word immediately before the number.\"\n    15\t    },\n    16\t    {\n    17\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    18\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    19\t      \"figures\": [\"F8\", \"F10\", \"F11\", \"F12\", \"F13\"],\n    20\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    21\t      \"why\": \"The rule requires \\\"one forecast figure for each quantity and period\\\" and allows \\\"outside figures beneath it.\\\" F8 is NVIDIA's guidance, which F11 uses to calculate a benchmark for choosing the Q4 growth rate. F10 is the sole Q3 company revenue forecast and supplies the revenue base for F13.\"\n    22\t    },\n    23\t    {\n    24\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    25\t      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    26\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F5\", \"F6\"],\n    27\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    28\t      \"why\": \"The reference rule allows computed figures but says the reference and the figures beneath it are \\\"never an estimate or a forecast.\\\" The $411 billion in e5204 is consensus for total company revenue, and F5 measures the segment's share in Q2. Applying that share to the full year requires the writer's own assumption about the FY2027 revenue mix, so F6's $380.24 billion is the writer's segment estimate.\"\n    29\t    }\n    30\t  ]\n    31\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/refusal-review/E-claude.claude.json\n     1\t{\"refusals\": [{\"message\": \"F4: \\\"$35 billion\\\" is part of the longer expression \\\"$35 billion to $170 billion\\\" in e4857; copy it whole\", \"stated\": \"no\", \"sentence\": \"None does. The closest is the quote_number description: \\\"a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression ... A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion'), given as low and high.\\\" Every example there is a range for one quantity, an uncertainty band to be given as low and high. The request never says that a start value and an end value of a change over time ('from $35 billion [in 2026] to $170 billion [in 2030]') count as one range that must be copied whole.\", \"right\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The quote says Bank of America believes 'the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion', in a report released in June 2026. So $35 billion is the CPU market's size, meaning its revenue, for 2026. F4 records exactly that: value 35 in USD bn (the scale billion is right), measure revenue, population CPU market, period CY2026, basis third_party, qualifier none. Copying the whole expression would set low 35 and high 170 for one 2026 figure. That would be wrong, because $170 billion is the 2030 value of a different period, not the top of a 2026 range.\"}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/refusal-review/E-claude.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"refusals\": [\n     3\t    {\n     4\t      \"message\": \"F4: \\\"$35 billion\\\" is part of the longer expression \\\"$35 billion to $170 billion\\\" in e4857; copy it whole\",\n     5\t      \"stated\": \"yes\",\n     6\t      \"sentence\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'.\",\n     7\t      \"right\": \"yes\",\n     8\t      \"why\": \"F4 correctly records Bank of America's forecast of 35 billion US dollars in CPU market revenue for calendar 2026. The passage gives two point forecasts for different years: $35 billion for 2026 and $170 billion for 2030. The rule against copying part of a range does not apply.\"\n     9\t    }\n    10\t  ]\n    11\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '440,490p;578,642p;695,746p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '440,490p;578,642p;695,746p'\"","aggregated_output":"   440\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{v} is not a member of the family'))\n   441\t    return errors\n   442\t\n   443\t\n   444\tdef _sources(passages, figs):\n   445\t    errors = []\n   446\t    for f in figs.values():\n   447\t        if f['kind'] != 'source':\n   448\t            continue\n   449\t        fid, qn, ev = f['id'], f['quote_number'], f['evidence']\n   450\t        if ev not in passages:\n   451\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: evidence_id {ev} is not a shown passage'))\n   452\t            continue\n   453\t        text = passages[ev]['text']\n   454\t        spans = expressions(text)\n   455\t        starts = [(s, e) for s, e in spans if text[s:e] == qn]\n   456\t        if not starts:\n   457\t            inside = [text[s:e] for s, e in spans if qn in text[s:e]]\n   458\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is part of the longer expression \"{inside[0]}\" in {ev}; copy it whole' if inside else\n   459\t                           f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is not written in the quote of {ev} as a complete number'))\n   460\t            continue\n   461\t        parsed = parse_number(qn)\n   462\t        if parsed is None:\n   463\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is not a US-dollar amount, a percentage or double, triple or twice, as written'))\n   464\t            continue\n   465\t        if f['unit'] != parsed['unit']:\n   466\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is in {parsed[\"unit\"]}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\n   467\t            continue\n   468\t        if 'low' in parsed:\n   469\t            ok = (_same(f.get('low'), parsed['low']) and _same(f.get('high'), parsed['high'])) or \\\n   470\t                 ('mid' in parsed and _same(f.get('value'), parsed['mid']))\n   471\t        else:\n   472\t            ok = _same(f.get('value'), parsed['value'])\n   473\t        if not ok:\n   474\t            want = (f'low {parsed[\"low\"]:g} and high {parsed[\"high\"]:g}' + (f', or value {parsed[\"mid\"]:g}' if 'mid' in parsed else '')\n   475\t                    if 'low' in parsed else f'value {parsed[\"value\"]:g}')\n   476\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" gives {want} {parsed[\"unit\"]}'))\n   477\t            continue\n   478\t        q = f.get('qualifier') or 'none'\n   479\t        written = []\n   480\t        for start, end in starts:\n   481\t            before, after = text[:start], text[end:]\n   482\t            found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_BEFORE if re.search(r'(?:^|\\W)' + re.escape(w) + r'\\s*$', before, re.I)), None)\n   483\t            if found is None:\n   484\t                found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_AFTER if re.match(r'^\\s*,?\\s*' + re.escape(w) + r'\\b', after, re.I)), None)\n   485\t            written.append(found or 'none')\n   486\t        if q not in written:\n   487\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: the quote writes \"{written[0] if written else \"none\"}\" with \"{qn}\", but the qualifier is {q}'))\n   488\t    return errors\n   489\t\n   490\t\n   578\t\n   579\tdef _adopted(outcomes, figs):\n   580\t    \"\"\"[54] The figures the outcomes adopt: each reached from an outcome (inputs and rests_on) with no figure of its own quantity and\n   581\t    period between them. A figure beneath another of its own quantity and period (a point a final range rests on) is its basis, not a\n   582\t    second value.\"\"\"\n   583\t    tops, seen_ids = set(), set()\n   584\t\n   585\t    def walk(fid, keys):\n   586\t        if fid not in figs or (fid, keys) in seen_ids:\n   587\t            return\n   588\t        seen_ids.add((fid, keys))\n   589\t        f = figs[fid]\n   590\t        k = _key(f['what'])\n   591\t        if k not in keys:\n   592\t            tops.add(fid)\n   593\t        for r in list(f.get('inputs') or []) + [r for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs]:\n   594\t            walk(r, keys | {k})\n   595\t    for out in outcomes.values():\n   596\t        walk(out, frozenset())\n   597\t    return tops\n   598\t\n   599\t\n   600\tdef _shared(family, figs, outcomes):\n   601\t    errors = []\n   602\t    by_key = {}\n   603\t    for fid in _adopted(outcomes, figs):                   # [54] forecasts the outcomes adopt; an unused alternative is not counted\n   604\t        if figs[fid]['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   605\t            by_key.setdefault(_key(figs[fid]['what']), []).append(fid)\n   606\t    nodes = {}\n   607\t    for fid, f in figs.items():\n   608\t        if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   609\t            nodes.setdefault(_key(f['what']), []).append(fid)\n   610\t    for k, ids in sorted(by_key.items()):\n   611\t        if len(ids) > 1:\n   612\t            errors.append(('S4', f'{\" | \".join(k)}: {len(ids)} forecasts ({\", \".join(sorted(ids))}); compute it once and use that figure'))\n   613\t    for want in family.get('shared', []):\n   614\t        key = _key(want)\n   615\t        node = by_key.get(key) or nodes.get(key, [])\n   616\t        for v, out in sorted(outcomes.items()):\n   617\t            reached, stack, seen_ids = [], [out], set()\n   618\t            while stack:\n   619\t                fid = stack.pop()\n   620\t                if fid in seen_ids or fid not in figs:\n   621\t                    continue\n   622\t                seen_ids.add(fid)\n   623\t                f = figs[fid]\n   624\t                if _key(f['what']) == key:\n   625\t                    reached.append(fid)\n   626\t                    if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   627\t                        continue                           # through the forecast figure: what lies beneath it is its basis\n   628\t                stack.extend(f.get('inputs') or [])\n   629\t            if not reached:\n   630\t                continue\n   631\t            if len(node) != 1:\n   632\t                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\" | \".join(key)} but the calculation has {len(node)} forecast figures for it'))\n   633\t                continue\n   634\t            bypass = sorted(r for r in reached if r != node[0])      # the walk never descends below the forecast figure\n   635\t            if bypass:\n   636\t                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\", \".join(bypass)} for {\" | \".join(key)} beside its forecast figure {node[0]}'))\n   637\t    return errors\n   638\t\n   639\t\n   640\tdef _part_whole(family, figs, calc, outcomes):\n   641\t    errors = []\n   642\t    members = family['members']\n   695\t        parsed = [_period(p) for p in periods]\n   696\t        if not any(parsed):\n   697\t            continue\n   698\t        if not all(parsed):\n   699\t            errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: when periods of one quantity are added or subtracted, write each as FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or '\n   700\t                                 f'FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise); got {\", \".join(periods)}'))\n   701\t            continue\n   702\t        years = {p[0] for p in parsed}\n   703\t        *parts_p, out = parsed\n   704\t        if len(years) != 1:\n   705\t            ok = False\n   706\t        elif f['operation'] == 'sum':\n   707\t            total = [q for p in parts_p for q in p[1]]\n   708\t            ok = len(total) == len(set(total)) and set(total) == out[1]\n   709\t        else:\n   710\t            ok = parts_p[1][1] <= parts_p[0][1] and parts_p[0][1] - parts_p[1][1] == out[1]\n   711\t        if not ok:\n   712\t            sign = ' + ' if f['operation'] == 'sum' else ' − '\n   713\t            errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: {sign.join(periods[:-1])} does not make up {periods[-1]}'))\n   714\t    return errors\n   715\t\n   716\t\n   717\tdef _recorded(family, figs, outcomes):\n   718\t    recorded = []\n   719\t    scopes = family.get('part_scopes', [])\n   720\t    for f in figs.values():\n   721\t        fid = f['id']\n   722\t        if f['kind'] == 'estimate':\n   723\t            recorded.append({'type': 'estimate', 'figure': fid, 'assumption': f.get('basis_text')})\n   724\t            rested = {figs[r]['what']['scope'] for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs}\n   725\t            if any({ps['part'], ps['whole']} <= rested for ps in scopes):\n   726\t                recorded.append({'type': 'part and whole in one estimate', 'figure': fid})\n   727\t        elif f['kind'] == 'computed':\n   728\t            money = [figs[i] for i in f['inputs'] if figs[i]['unit'] == 'USD bn']\n   729\t            if any(_quantity(i['what']) != _quantity(f['what']) for i in money) and f['operation'] != 'share':\n   730\t                recorded.append({'type': 'conversion between quantities', 'figure': fid})\n   731\t            if f['operation'] in ('apply_growth', 'product') and any(i['what']['period'] != f['what']['period'] for i in money):\n   732\t                recorded.append({'type': 'carried across periods', 'figure': fid})\n   733\t            if f['operation'] == 'sum' and any({ps['part'], ps['whole']} <= {i['what']['scope'] for i in money} for ps in scopes):\n   734\t                recorded.append({'type': 'part and whole added for different periods', 'figure': fid})\n   735\t        elif (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none':\n   736\t            recorded.append({'type': 'qualified source', 'figure': fid, 'qualifier': f['qualifier']})\n   737\t    used = set()\n   738\t    for out in outcomes.values():\n   739\t        used |= {out} | _beneath(out, figs)\n   740\t    by_key = {}\n   741\t    for fid in used:\n   742\t        by_key.setdefault(_key(figs[fid]['what']), []).append(fid)\n   743\t    for k, ids in sorted(by_key.items()):\n   744\t        if len(ids) > 1:\n   745\t            recorded.append({'type': 'one quantity, several figures', 'figure': sorted(ids)[0], 'figures': sorted(ids), 'what': list(k)})\n   746\t    return recorded\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/{P-v4,P-v8,P}/answers/codex.json; do printf '\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; rg -n '\"'\\\"id\\\"|growth rate|benchmark|growth implied|13%|\\\"rests_on\\\"|\\\"inputs\\\"|\\\"value\\\"|\\\"basis_text\\\"|\\\"operation\\\"|\\\"rung\\\"' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/{P-v4,P-v8,P}/answers/codex.json; do printf '\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; rg -n '\"'\\\"id\\\"|growth rate|benchmark|growth implied|13%|\\\"rests_on\\\"|\\\"inputs\\\"|\\\"value\\\"|\\\"basis_text\\\"|\\\"operation\\\"|\\\"rung\\\"' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/P-v4/answers/codex.json\n4:      \"id\": \"F1\",\n8:      \"value\": 411,\n15:      \"inputs\": null,\n16:      \"operation\": null,\n17:      \"basis_text\": null,\n18:      \"rests_on\": null,\n23:      \"id\": \"F2\",\n27:      \"value\": 81.62,\n34:      \"inputs\": null,\n35:      \"operation\": null,\n36:      \"basis_text\": null,\n37:      \"rests_on\": null,\n42:      \"id\": \"F3\",\n46:      \"value\": 96.22,\n53:      \"inputs\": null,\n54:      \"operation\": null,\n55:      \"basis_text\": null,\n56:      \"rests_on\": null,\n61:      \"id\": \"F4\",\n65:      \"value\": 108,\n72:      \"inputs\": null,\n73:      \"operation\": null,\n74:      \"basis_text\": null,\n75:      \"rests_on\": null,\n80:      \"id\": \"F5\",\n84:      \"value\": 113.6,\n91:      \"inputs\": null,\n92:      \"operation\": null,\n93:      \"basis_text\": null,\n94:      \"rests_on\": null,\n99:      \"id\": \"F6\",\n103:      \"value\": 113.6,\n110:      \"inputs\": null,\n111:      \"operation\": null,\n112:      \"basis_text\": \"I expect a beat in line with the independent estimate based on past guidance beats. The prior quarter's beat, Vera Rubin production shipments and CoreWeave's live deployment support that estimate. I keep the China Data Center compute assumption at zero for this quarter.\",\n113:      \"rests_on\": [\"F4\", \"F5\", \"e3200\", \"e2693\", \"e5118\"],\n118:      \"id\": \"F7\",\n122:      \"value\": 12.24,\n129:      \"inputs\": [\"F3\", \"F4\"],\n130:      \"operation\": \"growth\",\n131:      \"basis_text\": null,\n132:      \"rests_on\": null,\n137:      \"id\": \"F8\",\n141:      \"value\": 13,\n148:      \"inputs\": null,\n149:      \"operation\": null,\n150:      \"basis_text\": \"I carry forward the sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance and allow a modest increase as Rubin supply expands and hyperscaler sales reaccelerate. Memory, power and long delivery lead times limit how much demand can ship this quarter. The estimate allows a small contribution from limited H200 sales if China authorizes purchases in time.\",\n151:      \"rests_on\": [\"F7\", \"e3406\", \"e5067\", \"e4771\", \"e5205\"],\n156:      \"id\": \"F9\",\n160:      \"value\": 128.368,\n167:      \"inputs\": [\"F6\", \"F8\"],\n168:      \"operation\": \"apply_growth\",\n169:      \"basis_text\": null,\n170:      \"rests_on\": null,\n175:      \"id\": \"F10\",\n179:      \"value\": 419.808,\n186:      \"inputs\": [\"F2\", \"F3\", \"F6\", \"F9\"],\n187:      \"operation\": \"sum\",\n188:      \"basis_text\": null,\n189:      \"rests_on\": null,\n198:      \"rung\": 2,\n200:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $419.8 billion in FY2027 revenue, above the $411 billion analyst consensus. Both sit in rung 2. The two reported quarters contribute $81.62 billion and $96.22 billion. The prior guidance beat and actual Rubin deployments support a $113.6 billion Q3 estimate against NVIDIA's $108 billion guidance midpoint. I carry the growth implied by that guidance into Q4 and allow a modest lift from the expected pickup in hyperscaler sales as Rubin supply builds, yielding $128.4 billion for Q4. Memory and power constraints and long delivery times keep the forecast below the top rung. The AWS deployment commitment spans several years, so only near-term deliveries can contribute. The capex themes support demand, with the stronger calendar 2027 outlook bearing on FY2027 primarily through near-term orders and January deliveries. Limited H200 authorization adds modest late-year upside because approvals still need to turn into shipments. The other policy themes imply no change large enough to alter the quarter's shipment forecast.\",\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/codex.json\n4:      \"id\": \"F1\",\n8:      \"value\": 75.25,\n15:      \"inputs\": null,\n16:      \"operation\": null,\n17:      \"basis_text\": null,\n18:      \"rests_on\": null,\n23:      \"id\": \"F2\",\n27:      \"value\": 89,\n34:      \"inputs\": null,\n35:      \"operation\": null,\n36:      \"basis_text\": null,\n37:      \"rests_on\": null,\n42:      \"id\": \"F3\",\n46:      \"value\": 96.2,\n53:      \"inputs\": null,\n54:      \"operation\": null,\n55:      \"basis_text\": null,\n56:      \"rests_on\": null,\n61:      \"id\": \"F4\",\n65:      \"value\": 411,\n72:      \"inputs\": null,\n73:      \"operation\": null,\n74:      \"basis_text\": null,\n75:      \"rests_on\": null,\n80:      \"id\": \"F5\",\n84:      \"value\": 0.92515593,\n91:      \"inputs\": [\"F2\", \"F3\"],\n92:      \"operation\": \"ratio\",\n93:      \"basis_text\": null,\n94:      \"rests_on\": null,\n99:      \"id\": \"F6\",\n103:      \"value\": 380.24,\n110:      \"inputs\": [\"F4\", \"F5\"],\n111:      \"operation\": \"product\",\n112:      \"basis_text\": null,\n113:      \"rests_on\": null,\n118:      \"id\": \"F7\",\n122:      \"value\": 361,\n129:      \"inputs\": null,\n130:      \"operation\": null,\n131:      \"basis_text\": null,\n132:      \"rests_on\": null,\n137:      \"id\": \"F8\",\n141:      \"value\": 108,\n148:      \"inputs\": null,\n149:      \"operation\": null,\n150:      \"basis_text\": null,\n151:      \"rests_on\": null,\n156:      \"id\": \"F9\",\n160:      \"value\": 113.6,\n167:      \"inputs\": null,\n168:      \"operation\": null,\n169:      \"basis_text\": null,\n170:      \"rests_on\": null,\n175:      \"id\": \"F10\",\n179:      \"value\": 112.5,\n186:      \"inputs\": null,\n187:      \"operation\": null,\n188:      \"basis_text\": \"I expect a beat on official guidance. The prior quarter exceeded its outlook, and production shipments and a live customer deployment support a further step-up. Supply limits lead me to allow less upside than the outside estimate based on past guidance beats. This quarter assumes no China Data Center compute contribution.\",\n189:      \"rests_on\": [\"F8\", \"F9\", \"e3200\", \"e2693\", \"e5118\", \"e5067\"],\n194:      \"id\": \"F11\",\n198:      \"value\": 12.2661,\n205:      \"inputs\": [\"F3\", \"F8\"],\n206:      \"operation\": \"growth\",\n207:      \"basis_text\": null,\n208:      \"rests_on\": null,\n213:      \"id\": \"F12\",\n217:      \"value\": 13,\n224:      \"inputs\": null,\n225:      \"operation\": null,\n226:      \"basis_text\": \"I carry the guided Q3 sequential pace into Q4 with a small increase. Management expects hyperscale growth to pick up as Rubin supply expands. The estimate includes a small allowance for late H200 shipments if authorized. Memory availability and channel lead times limit the increase.\",\n227:      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"e3406\", \"e2693\", \"e5118\", \"e5067\", \"e5205\", \"e2652\"],\n232:      \"id\": \"F13\",\n236:      \"value\": 127.125,\n243:      \"inputs\": [\"F10\", \"F12\"],\n244:      \"operation\": \"apply_growth\",\n245:      \"basis_text\": null,\n246:      \"rests_on\": null,\n251:      \"id\": \"F14\",\n255:      \"value\": 239.625,\n262:      \"inputs\": [\"F10\", \"F13\"],\n263:      \"operation\": \"sum\",\n264:      \"basis_text\": null,\n265:      \"rests_on\": null,\n270:      \"id\": \"F15\",\n274:      \"value\": 93,\n281:      \"inputs\": null,\n282:      \"operation\": null,\n283:      \"basis_text\": \"I expect the second-half Data Center share to rise slightly from Q2. NVIDIA expects Data Center to drive most near-term revenue growth, with AI cloud, industrial and enterprise customers leading in Q3 and hyperscalers picking up in Q4.\",\n284:      \"rests_on\": [\"F5\", \"e5223\", \"e5061\", \"e3406\"],\n289:      \"id\": \"F16\",\n293:      \"value\": 222.85125,\n300:      \"inputs\": [\"F14\", \"F15\"],\n301:      \"operation\": \"share\",\n302:      \"basis_text\": null,\n303:      \"rests_on\": null,\n308:      \"id\": \"F17\",\n312:      \"value\": 164.25,\n319:      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n320:      \"operation\": \"sum\",\n321:      \"basis_text\": null,\n322:      \"rests_on\": null,\n327:      \"id\": \"F18\",\n331:      \"value\": 387.10125,\n338:      \"inputs\": [\"F17\", \"F16\"],\n339:      \"operation\": \"sum\",\n340:      \"basis_text\": null,\n341:      \"rests_on\": null,\n350:      \"rung\": 2,\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/P/answers/codex.json\n4:      \"id\": \"F1\",\n6:      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"consensus\", \"value\": 411, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n8:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n12:      \"id\": \"F2\",\n14:      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 361, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n16:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n20:      \"id\": \"F3\",\n22:      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 81.62, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n24:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n28:      \"id\": \"F4\",\n30:      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 96.22, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n32:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n36:      \"id\": \"F5\",\n38:      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 108, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n40:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n44:      \"id\": \"F6\",\n46:      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 113.6, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n48:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n52:      \"id\": \"F7\",\n54:      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 113, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n56:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n57:      \"basis_text\": \"I expect a beat against NVIDIA's guidance given the recent pattern of beats, the strength in AI cloud and enterprise sales, and Rubin shipments already underway. I use a smaller beat than Pradhan's historical extrapolation because channel lead times and component shortages limit extra shipments.\",\n58:      \"rests_on\": [\"F5\", \"F6\", \"e3200\", \"e5061\", \"e5118\", \"e5067\", \"e5205\"],\n62:      \"id\": \"F8\",\n64:      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 12, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n66:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n70:      \"id\": \"F9\",\n72:      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 12, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n74:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n75:      \"basis_text\": \"I carry the growth implied by Q3 guidance forward from my higher Q3 base. Expanding Rubin supply and recovering hyperscaler growth support that pace. Memory and infrastructure constraints limit further acceleration. I allow for a small contribution from H200 sales if authorization and deliveries arrive in time.\",\n76:      \"rests_on\": [\"F7\", \"F8\", \"e3406\", \"e5067\", \"e5205\", \"e2652\"],\n80:      \"id\": \"F10\",\n82:      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 126.56, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n84:      \"inputs\": [\"F7\", \"F9\"], \"operation\": \"apply_growth\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n88:      \"id\": \"F11\",\n90:      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 75.25, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n92:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n96:      \"id\": \"F12\",\n98:      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 89.02, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n100:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n104:      \"id\": \"F13\",\n106:      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 92.5, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n108:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n109:      \"basis_text\": \"I hold the segment near its reported Q2 share. AI Clouds, Industrial and Enterprise drive most of the incremental Data Center sales, while growth outside Data Center also continues.\",\n110:      \"rests_on\": [\"F4\", \"F12\", \"e3399\", \"e5059\", \"e5061\"],\n114:      \"id\": \"F14\",\n116:      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 104.525, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n118:      \"inputs\": [\"F7\", \"F13\"], \"operation\": \"share\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n122:      \"id\": \"F15\",\n124:      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 93, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n126:      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n127:      \"basis_text\": \"I expect Data Center to take a slightly larger share as hyperscaler growth picks up and Rubin shipments expand. Any H200 sales recognized in the quarter are included in this segment share and in the company forecast.\",\n128:      \"rests_on\": [\"F13\", \"e3399\", \"e3406\", \"e5061\", \"e5118\"],\n132:      \"id\": \"F16\",\n134:      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 117.7008, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n136:      \"inputs\": [\"F10\", \"F15\"], \"operation\": \"share\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n140:      \"id\": \"F17\",\n142:      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 417.4, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n144:      \"inputs\": [\"F3\", \"F4\", \"F7\", \"F10\"], \"operation\": \"sum\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n148:      \"id\": \"F18\",\n150:      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 386.5, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n152:      \"inputs\": [\"F11\", \"F12\", \"F14\", \"F16\"], \"operation\": \"sum\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n160:      \"rung\": 2,\n162:      \"reasons\": \"The $411 billion consensus is in rung 2. I forecast $417.4 billion. After the reported first half, I expect $113 billion in Q3 against the $108 billion guidance midpoint. The recent guidance beats and CoreWeave's live Rubin deployment support that increase. Carrying the guided 12% sequential growth rate forward gives $126.56 billion in Q4 as Rubin supply expands and hyperscaler growth picks up. Memory constraints and partner lead times limit the size of the beat. Hyperscaler spending supports demand, with the updated calendar 2027 outlook mainly affecting later quarters. Limited H200 authorization offers some Q4 upside if shipments can land in time. The resulting forecast is above consensus and within rung 2.\",\n187:      \"rung\": 2,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
