{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'four largest|three|total capex|data.center capex|\\\"id\\\"|proxy' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json; rg -n 'C5|392B consensus' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md; rg -n '80|90|outcome' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | awk -F: '\"'$1>=11122 && $1<11156'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"1:{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Alphabet\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"company total, 2026 guidance\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":null,\"low\":195,\"high\":205,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1254\",\"quote_number\":\"$195 billion to $205 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"company total, FY26 guidance (fiscal year equals calendar year)\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":null,\"low\":125,\"high\":145,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1549\",\"quote_number\":\"$125 billion to $145 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"company total, raised 2026 projection\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":220,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1649\",\"quote_number\":\"$220 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F4\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Alphabet, Meta Platforms and Amazon\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined guidance of three of the four\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":null,\"low\":540,\"high\":570,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\",\"F3\"],\"operation\":\"sum\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F5\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"four largest AI hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"data-center capex\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":null,\"low\":720,\"high\":745,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e5014\",\"quote_number\":\"$720 billion to $745 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"roughly\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[\"v6\"]},{\"id\":\"F6\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"implied: combined four-company tally less the other three companies' guidance\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":null,\"low\":150,\"high\":205,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F5\",\"F4\"],\"operation\":\"difference\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F7\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"company total, quarter ended March 2026\",\"period\":\"FY2026 Q3\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":30.88,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e922\",\"quote_number\":\"$30.88 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F8\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"company total\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":165,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My estimate. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 capex figure in the evidence. Its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% year over year. If quarterly spending keeps rising (supported by the $678bn Azure backlog), the four calendar-2026 quarters add up to about $145-155bn, not the $124bn a flat annualised quarter would give. The combined tally minus the other three companies' guidance implies $150-205bn. I take $165bn, in the lower half of that implied range, because the reported run-rate does not support the top of it.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F6\",\"F7\",\"e922\",\"e4405\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F9\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"four largest AI hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"data-center capex\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":720,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\",\"F3\",\"F8\"],\"operation\":\"sum\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[\"v6\"],\"reference_for\":[]}],\"answers\":[{\"view_id\":\"v6\",\"shape\":\"forecast\",\"rung\":4,\"reference_unavailable\":null,\"reasons\":\"Reference: the market expects the four companies (Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta) to spend roughly $720-745bn combined in 2026 (Zacks, e5014; the same range is reported as their combined guidance, e2079). That range sits in rung 4 ($700-800bn). Bottom-up build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn after raising its guidance (e1254). Meta guides $125-145bn after raising its guidance (e1549). Amazon raised its projection to $220bn (e1649). Microsoft has no calendar-2026 guidance in the evidence. Its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922). Carrying that growth forward gives about $145-155bn for the year, and the combined tally minus the other three's guidance implies $150-205bn, so I estimate $165bn. The sum is $705-735bn, midpoint $720bn, which is rung 4 at every point of the range. Upside: all three companies that guide have raised during the year; memory costs are lifting dollar spending (e4567); demand stays strong (Azure backlog of $678bn, e4405; Google Cloud growth of 82%, e4406); independent tallies run at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); and NVIDIA's estimate of nearly $800bn for the top five (e2759) implies the four in the mid-$700s once the fifth company is taken out. Reaching rung 5 would still need about $80bn more than my build, and no company has guided to that. Downside: sources measure total capex, and part of it is not data-center spending (for example, Amazon's logistics); the $130bn of US projects blocked or delayed (e122) is not specific to these four companies and is not sized against their spending; and the shortages of electricians and memory chips (e1545, e4566) are constraints with no size attached. Falling below $700bn would take guidance cuts that no evidence shows. Opinions on financing risk (e2081, e4807) report no change to anyone's capex. Rung 4 holds.\",\"why\":null,\"cites\":[\"e1254\",\"e1549\",\"e1649\",\"e922\",\"e5014\",\"e2079\",\"e1947\",\"e1856\",\"e2458\",\"e2759\",\"e5055\",\"e4405\",\"e4406\",\"e4567\",\"e122\",\"e1545\",\"e4566\",\"e2081\",\"e4807\"],\"pushes\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1254\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1549\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1649\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e922\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5014\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2079\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1947\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1856\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2458\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2759\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5055\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4405\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4406\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4567\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e122\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1545\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4566\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2081\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4807\",\"push\":\"none\"}],\"theme_pushes\":[]}]}\n75:| **C5 reference** [46b.3, 46c.1] | each answer's reference is `unavailable` with a reason, or a direct `source` figure of basis consensus, guidance or third_party from a passage whose speaker is not the system, or a `computed` figure whose whole graph is computed figures over such sources and `reported` sources (q02: $392B consensus × 92% reported mix). No `estimate`, `forecast` or `prior_forecast` anywhere in the reference's graph. Its provenance is shown | the system's own forecast presented as the market's, directly or wrapped in an estimate that cites outside evidence |\n82:| **C5 one choice** [46g.2] | a reference names exactly one of a figure or `unavailable` | a prior figure hidden beside an \"unavailable\" reason |\n83:| **C9 same base** [46f.3, 46j.2] | for each `same_base(later, earlier)`, the later member rests on the earlier member's forecast nodes (its outcome, or the scenario paths behind its range summary), and every path to a figure of the earlier quantity and period passes through one of them; scenarios match by C4 | FY2028 grown from the $392B consensus instead of q01's $400–435B forecast |\n162:| G1–G4 (controls) | q01, q02, q19, q22, from `gold-v1.json`'s reasoning (expressions from the packet items). q01's Q1 + Q2 as `time_aggregation`; q02's comparison $392B (consensus) × 92% (reported mix); q22 in one family with q01, reading q01's FY2027 node | all pass, estimates visible; q02's reference passes C5; q19's overlapping range passes C6 with its reason |\n163:| P1–P3 (plants, on control copies) | P1, an amount absent from its expression; P2, a wrong sum; P3a, a prior forecast as the reference; P3b, the same figure wrapped in an estimate that cites outside evidence | P1 **C1**, P2 **C2**, P3a and P3b **C5**, each for its intended reason |\n11126:- **Registered result (D725's lines, scored as written, D680):** all 72 answers refused by `calc_check.py`, none published; the joint\n11132:  - 0 of 90 member answers named a figure as the outcome; the model wrote the number in words (\"$398.1 billion\");\n11137:  - The format's rules live in the check, not in the request. The sent schema has 7 field descriptions. `evidence`, `outcome`,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R{02,05,07,08,09}.{claude,codex}.json; do printf '\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; rg -n '\"'supported|decision|why|type|figures' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R{02,05,07,08,09}.{claude,codex}.json; do printf '\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; rg -n '\"'supported|decision|why|type|figures' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R02.claude.json\n4:   \"supported\": \"yes\"\n10:   \"type\": \"number misread\",\n11:   \"figures\": [\n14:   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n15:   \"why\": \"quote writes 'an additional $1 billion'; 'up to' belongs to the $3 billion total\"\n19:   \"type\": \"number misread\",\n20:   \"figures\": [\n23:   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n24:   \"why\": \"quote 'about $70 billion' matches value, scale and qualifier\"\n28:   \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n29:   \"figures\": [\n34:   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n35:   \"why\": \"42 + 25 = 67; qualifier on F8 does not make the sum wrong\"\n39:   \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n40:   \"figures\": [\n45:   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n46:   \"why\": \"F3 = difference of a reported and a guidance source; nothing beneath is an estimate or forecast\"\n49: \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the Claude adjudicator hand-back (decisions and figures verbatim; reasons shortened).\"\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R02.codex.json\n4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n5:      \"why\": \"The forecast treats the $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027 as part of the $25 billion July balance and assumes it is paid by year-end, leaving $7 billion. The low case adds $1 billion for Lancium. The high case also adds $1.5 billion for SB Energy, $10 billion for Anthropic, and a stated $4.5 billion allowance for other deals or delays. Those assumptions yield $8–24 billion, entirely within rung 1. The disclosures do not establish the assumed payment schedule.\"\n11:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n12:      \"figures\": [\"F4\"],\n13:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n14:      \"why\": \"The e1919 quote says “up to $3 billion” for the full deal and “an additional $1 billion” for the milestone tranche. The format takes the numeral and its qualifier from the quote. The unsupported Q3 timing does not change either.\"\n18:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n19:      \"figures\": [\"F7\"],\n20:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n21:      \"why\": \"The e2080 quote says “about $70 billion in equity investments.” F7 preserves the amount, scale, and qualifier. The quote does not establish the Q3 timing or the paid-and-unpaid interpretation, but those claims do not change the quoted number.\"\n25:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n26:      \"figures\": [\"F8\", \"F1\", \"F9\"],\n27:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n28:      \"why\": \"The format says “The value is the number as written” and records the source qualifier separately. F8 correctly stores 42 with “more than.” The sum of the two recorded point values is 42 + 25 = 67. In prose, the underlying total is more than $67 billion.\"\n32:      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n33:      \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\"],\n34:      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n35:      \"why\": \"The reference rule says the figure and every figure beneath it may be “never an estimate or a forecast.” E2889 says “$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.” It does not say that the $18 billion is part of the July 26 balance or that it will all be paid by year-end. F3 relies on both assumptions, so its $7 billion cannot be attributed to NVIDIA's own schedule.\"\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R05.claude.json\n4:   \"supported\": \"yes\"\n10:   \"type\": \"number misread\",\n11:   \"figures\": [\n14:   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n15:   \"why\": \"midpoint value of a plus-or-minus expression is a permitted form; no qualifier before the number\"\n19:   \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n20:   \"figures\": [\n23:   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n24:   \"why\": \"F13 = consensus source x ratio of two reported sources; nothing beneath is an estimate or forecast\"\n27: \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the Claude adjudicator hand-back (decisions and figures verbatim; reasons shortened). Full text in the session record.\"\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R05.codex.json\n4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n5:      \"why\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue totals $164.25 billion (e109, e2650). The $113 billion Q3 company forecast assumes a 2.6% beat above the top of guidance, below the recent 3.1% average given supply constraints (e2652, e5055, e4911, e3733). Carrying Q3's guided 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives $126.6 billion, with the expected pickup in hyperscale growth supporting that assumption (e4899, e3406). Holding the reported Q2 Data Center share at 92.5% gives $104.5 billion in Q3 and $117.1 billion in Q4. The full-year total rounds to $385.9 billion, within rung 2's $350 billion to below $400 billion band.\"\n11:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n12:      \"figures\": [\"F5\"],\n13:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n14:      \"why\": \"F5 quotes e2652's full \\\"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\\\" expression. The format expressly permits a plus-or-minus expression to be given as \\\"low and high or its midpoint as value.\\\" The plus-or-minus wording is part of the quoted expression, and there is no separate qualifier immediately before the number.\"\n18:      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n19:      \"figures\": [\"F13\"],\n20:      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n21:      \"why\": \"The reference rule says the figure and every figure beneath it must be \\\"never an estimate or a forecast.\\\" In e5204, analysts \\\"estimate will make $411 billion in revenue for its current fiscal year,\\\" which is a company-wide figure. F4 measures the Data Center share for Q2 alone. Using that share for the full fiscal year to produce $380.2 billion relies on the writer's own assumption about the annual revenue mix.\"\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.claude.json\n1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The forecast follows from the passages shown and the cut the answer states openly. F1 = 20 comes from e3660, which says Kress 'still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027'. F2 = 2x with the qualifier 'more than' comes from 'expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028'. F3 = product(20, 2) = 40 USD bn, and the format rules allow USD bn × x. F8 is an estimate, which the format rules define as 'your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text'. Its basis_text gives the assumption: 'the guided floor of more than $40 billion (F3), cut by about 15%'. 40 × 0.85 = 34, and 34 falls in rung 3 ('at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion'). Each reason for the cut can be found in a passage. Deployment is written in the future tense: 'the Vera CPU will be deployed by' (e4689). Anthropic 'is evaluating its Vera CPU' (e5044). The FY2027 base is an expectation ('expects $20 billion', e3660), not a reported figure. On Reader 1's scope objection: BofA's own figures, 'from $35 billion to $170 billion' between 2026 and 2030 (e4857), imply about 48.5% growth a year. That puts 2027 at about $52 billion, close to the answer's F6 of 52.5, so applying AMD's 50% rate does not change the cross-check in any way that matters. The answer also notes 'even allowing for different market definitions'. On the open-ended guidance: forecasting below the guided floor is the estimate's stated judgement, which the format rules allow; it is not a step that fails to follow from the passages. The size of the cut is an assumption the reader can check, not something the answer claims to derive.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json\n4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n5:      \"why\": \"The $20 billion outlook and 'more than double' imply a $40 billion lower boundary. The writer uses that boundary as the starting point and assumes a 15% discount, yielding $34 billion in rung 3. The phrases 'will be deployed' and 'is evaluating' support the direction of the discount. The 15% is the writer’s stated judgment. The $52.5 billion cross-check combines Bank of America’s 'CPU market' baseline with AMD’s 'server CPU market' growth rate, so the passages do not establish that comparison.\"\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R08.claude.json\n4:   \"supported\": \"yes\"\n7:   \"supported\": \"yes\"\n13:   \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n14:   \"figures\": [\n17:   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n18:   \"why\": \"F17 = share of quoted consensus and reported share; no estimate or forecast beneath\"\n21: \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the Claude adjudicator hand-back (decisions and figures verbatim; reasons shortened). Full text in the session record.\"\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R08.codex.json\n4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n5:      \"why\": \"NVIDIA reported $81.62B in Q1 and $96.2B in Q2. The $112.5B Q3 forecast starts from guidance of $108.0B, plus or minus 2%, and allows for a beat given the prior 12 quarters, tempered by capacity limits. Carrying the guided 12% sequential growth rate into Q4 is a stated assumption supported by the expected pickup in hyperscale growth. That gives $126.0B for Q4 and $416.32B for the year, within rung 2.\"\n8:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n9:      \"why\": \"NVIDIA reported $75.25B in Q1 and $89.0B in Q2. The answer holds Data Center at its latest share of roughly 92.5% for Q3 and Q4. Applying that assumption to the shared company forecasts gives $104.06B and $116.55B. The four quarters total $384.86B, within rung 2, and the segment stays below the company total in each period.\"\n15:      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n16:      \"figures\": [\"F17\"],\n17:      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n18:      \"why\": \"The format defines consensus as \\\"analysts' average\\\" and requires the market reference and its inputs to be \\\"never an estimate or a forecast.\\\" The $411B is company-wide consensus, and e5116 says 92.5% is the share of \\\"Nvidia's latest quarterly sales.\\\" Applying that quarterly share to the full year is the writer's assumption, which makes the $380.18B an estimate of segment revenue.\"\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R09.claude.json\n4:   \"supported\": \"yes\"\n10:   \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n11:   \"figures\": [\n15:   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n16:   \"why\": \"outcome key matches; company capex vs data-center capex is a support question, and passages use the terms interchangeably (e5376 vs e5081)\"\n20:   \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n21:   \"figures\": [\n24:   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n25:   \"why\": \"midpoint of 705-735 is permitted\"\n28: \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the Claude adjudicator hand-back (decisions and figures verbatim; reasons shortened). Full text in the session record.\"\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R09.codex.json\n4:      \"supported\": \"no\",\n5:      \"why\": \"The calculation gives a $720 billion midpoint for total capital spending. The answer acknowledges that some of that spending, such as Amazon's logistics spending, falls outside data centers. It gives no estimate or bound for that share. More than $20 billion outside data centers would put the forecast below rung 4, even if all four companies meet their total spending projections.\"\n11:      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n12:      \"figures\": [\"F5\", \"F9\"],\n13:      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n14:      \"why\": \"e5014 says the four companies will spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion 'on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.' F5 labels that company-wide total as data-center capex. F9 also sums company-wide spending without an adjustment, although the format rule says the outcome's 'what is the member's outcome key,' which specifies data-center capex.\"\n18:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n19:      \"figures\": [\"F9\"],\n20:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n21:      \"why\": \"F1, F2, F3 and F8 sum to $705–735 billion, whose midpoint is $720 billion. The operation rule explicitly says 'you may declare the midpoint as value,' so F9 can report $720 billion with sum as its declared operation.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom collections import Counter\nimport json,hashlib\np=Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\ndef read(path): return json.loads(path.read_text())\nchecked={(x['\\\\''case'\\\\''],x['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']):x for x in read(p/'\\\\''CHECKED.json'\\\\'')}\nkey=read(p/'\\\\''reader-key.json'\\\\'')\ntally={x[0]:x[1:] for x in read(p/'\\\\''TALLY.json'\\\\'')}\nprint('\\\\''stages'\\\\'',Counter((x['\\\\''stage'\\\\''],x['\\\\''decision'\\\\'']) for x in checked.values()))\nprint('\\\\''inventories: answers, readers, adjudications'\\\\'',len(list(p.glob('\\\\''*/answers/*.json'\\\\''))),len(list(p.glob('\\\\''reader-packets/R*.json'\\\\''))),len(list(p.glob('\\\\''adjudication/R*.json'\\\\''))))\nfinal=Counter(); defects=Counter(); labels=Counter()\nfor rid,k in key.items():\n    c,w=k['\\\\''case'\\\\''],k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']; ch=checked[c,w]\n    ap=p/c/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{w}.json'\\\\''; a=read(ap)\n    print(rid,c,w, '\\\\''SHA'\\\\'',ch['\\\\''sha256'\\\\'']==hashlib.sha256(ap.read_bytes()).hexdigest(),{x['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:(x['\\\\''rung'\\\\''],ch.get('\\\\''published'\\\\'',{}).get(x['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''])) for x in a['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']},ch['\\\\''errors'\\\\''])\n    rd,ad=[[read(q) for q in sorted((p/d).glob(rid+'\\\\''.*.json'\\\\''))] for d in ('\\\\''reader-packets'\\\\'','\\\\''adjudication'\\\\'')]\n    active=ad or rd\n    print('\\\\''  readers:'\\\\'',[([v['\\\\''supported'\\\\''] for v in x['\\\\''members'\\\\''].values()],[d['\\\\''type'\\\\''] for d in x['\\\\''defects'\\\\'']]) for x in rd])\n    print('\\\\''  settle:'\\\\'',[([v['\\\\''supported'\\\\''] for v in x['\\\\''members'\\\\''].values()],[(d['\\\\''type'\\\\''],d['\\\\''decision'\\\\'']) for d in x['\\\\''defects'\\\\'']]) for x in ad])\n    for m in active[0]['\\\\''members'\\\\'']:\n        final[tuple(x['\\\\''members'\\\\''][m]['\\\\''supported'\\\\''] for x in active)]+=1\n    if ad:\n        for ctype, decisions in tally[rid][-1].items():\n            defects[(ctype, tuple(sorted(set(x.split('\\\\'':'\\\\'')[1] for x in decisions))))]+=1\n            labels[(ctype, tuple(sorted(set(x.split('\\\\'':'\\\\'')[1] for x in decisions))))]+=len(decisions)//2\n    for f in a['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n        if f['\\\\''kind'\\\\'']=='\\\\''estimate'\\\\'' and f['\\\\''unit'\\\\'']=='\\\\''%'\\\\'' and f['\\\\''what'\\\\'']['\\\\''period'\\\\'']=='\\\\''FY2027 Q4'\\\\'':\n            print('\\\\''  q4 rate:'\\\\'',f['\\\\''id'\\\\''],f['\\\\''value'\\\\''],f['\\\\''rests_on'\\\\''],f['\\\\''basis_text'\\\\''])\nprint('\\\\''final support'\\\\'', final)\nprint('\\\\''defect packet/type counts'\\\\'',defects)\nprint('\\\\''defect individual claim counts'\\\\'',labels)\nfor f in sorted(p.glob('\\\\''*/codex-writer.stream'\\\\'')):\n    print(f.parent.name)\n    for line in f.open():\n        x=json.loads(line); item=x.get('\\\\''item'\\\\'',{})\n        if x.get('\\\\''type'\\\\'')=='\\\\''item.completed'\\\\'' and item.get('\\\\''type'\\\\'')=='\\\\''command_execution'\\\\'': print(item.get('\\\\''command'\\\\''))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom collections import Counter\nimport json,hashlib\np=Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\ndef read(path): return json.loads(path.read_text())\nchecked={(x['\\\\''case'\\\\''],x['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']):x for x in read(p/'\\\\''CHECKED.json'\\\\'')}\nkey=read(p/'\\\\''reader-key.json'\\\\'')\ntally={x[0]:x[1:] for x in read(p/'\\\\''TALLY.json'\\\\'')}\nprint('\\\\''stages'\\\\'',Counter((x['\\\\''stage'\\\\''],x['\\\\''decision'\\\\'']) for x in checked.values()))\nprint('\\\\''inventories: answers, readers, adjudications'\\\\'',len(list(p.glob('\\\\''*/answers/*.json'\\\\''))),len(list(p.glob('\\\\''reader-packets/R*.json'\\\\''))),len(list(p.glob('\\\\''adjudication/R*.json'\\\\''))))\nfinal=Counter(); defects=Counter(); labels=Counter()\nfor rid,k in key.items():\n    c,w=k['\\\\''case'\\\\''],k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']; ch=checked[c,w]\n    ap=p/c/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{w}.json'\\\\''; a=read(ap)\n    print(rid,c,w, '\\\\''SHA'\\\\'',ch['\\\\''sha256'\\\\'']==hashlib.sha256(ap.read_bytes()).hexdigest(),{x['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:(x['\\\\''rung'\\\\''],ch.get('\\\\''published'\\\\'',{}).get(x['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''])) for x in a['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']},ch['\\\\''errors'\\\\''])\n    rd,ad=[[read(q) for q in sorted((p/d).glob(rid+'\\\\''.*.json'\\\\''))] for d in ('\\\\''reader-packets'\\\\'','\\\\''adjudication'\\\\'')]\n    active=ad or rd\n    print('\\\\''  readers:'\\\\'',[([v['\\\\''supported'\\\\''] for v in x['\\\\''members'\\\\''].values()],[d['\\\\''type'\\\\''] for d in x['\\\\''defects'\\\\'']]) for x in rd])\n    print('\\\\''  settle:'\\\\'',[([v['\\\\''supported'\\\\''] for v in x['\\\\''members'\\\\''].values()],[(d['\\\\''type'\\\\''],d['\\\\''decision'\\\\'']) for d in x['\\\\''defects'\\\\'']]) for x in ad])\n    for m in active[0]['\\\\''members'\\\\'']:\n        final[tuple(x['\\\\''members'\\\\''][m]['\\\\''supported'\\\\''] for x in active)]+=1\n    if ad:\n        for ctype, decisions in tally[rid][-1].items():\n            defects[(ctype, tuple(sorted(set(x.split('\\\\'':'\\\\'')[1] for x in decisions))))]+=1\n            labels[(ctype, tuple(sorted(set(x.split('\\\\'':'\\\\'')[1] for x in decisions))))]+=len(decisions)//2\n    for f in a['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n        if f['\\\\''kind'\\\\'']=='\\\\''estimate'\\\\'' and f['\\\\''unit'\\\\'']=='\\\\''%'\\\\'' and f['\\\\''what'\\\\'']['\\\\''period'\\\\'']=='\\\\''FY2027 Q4'\\\\'':\n            print('\\\\''  q4 rate:'\\\\'',f['\\\\''id'\\\\''],f['\\\\''value'\\\\''],f['\\\\''rests_on'\\\\''],f['\\\\''basis_text'\\\\''])\nprint('\\\\''final support'\\\\'', final)\nprint('\\\\''defect packet/type counts'\\\\'',defects)\nprint('\\\\''defect individual claim counts'\\\\'',labels)\nfor f in sorted(p.glob('\\\\''*/codex-writer.stream'\\\\'')):\n    print(f.parent.name)\n    for line in f.open():\n        x=json.loads(line); item=x.get('\\\\''item'\\\\'',{})\n        if x.get('\\\\''type'\\\\'')=='\\\\''item.completed'\\\\'' and item.get('\\\\''type'\\\\'')=='\\\\''command_execution'\\\\'': print(item.get('\\\\''command'\\\\''))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 36, in <module>\n  File \"/Users/lchingen/.pyenv/versions/3.10.10/lib/python3.10/json/__init__.py\", line 346, in loads\n    return _default_decoder.decode(s)\n  File \"/Users/lchingen/.pyenv/versions/3.10.10/lib/python3.10/json/decoder.py\", line 340, in decode\n    raise JSONDecodeError(\"Extra data\", s, end)\njson.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Extra data: line 1 column 5 (char 4)\nstages Counter({('checked', 'publish'): 11, ('checked', 'refused'): 1})\ninventories: answers, readers, adjudications 12 24 18\nR01 E codex SHA True {'v41': (3, [36.0, 36.0, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes'], []), (['yes'], [])]\n  settle: []\nR02 O claude SHA True {'v29': (1, [8.0, 24.0, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes'], []), (['no'], ['number misread', 'number misread', 'wrong arithmetic', \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\"])]\n  settle: [(['yes'], [('number misread', 'reject'), ('number misread', 'reject'), ('wrong arithmetic', 'reject'), (\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", 'reject')]), (['yes'], [('number misread', 'reject'), ('number misread', 'reject'), ('wrong arithmetic', 'reject'), (\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", 'uphold')])]\nR03 P-v4 claude SHA True {'v4': (2, [417.38, 417.38, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes'], []), (['yes'], [])]\n  settle: []\nR04 S codex SHA True {'v6': (4, [715.0, 715.0, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes'], []), (['yes'], [])]\n  settle: []\nR05 P-v8 claude SHA True {'v8': (2, [385.88035343035347, 385.88035343035347, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes'], []), (['yes'], ['number misread', \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\"])]\n  settle: [(['yes'], [('number misread', 'reject'), (\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", 'reject')]), (['yes'], [('number misread', 'reject'), (\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", 'uphold')])]\nR06 P-v4 codex SHA True {'v4': (2, [419.808, 419.808, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes'], ['two forecasts of one quarter']), (['yes'], ['number misread', 'two forecasts of one quarter'])]\n  settle: [(['yes'], [('two forecasts of one quarter', 'uphold'), ('number misread', 'reject'), ('two forecasts of one quarter', 'uphold')]), (['yes'], [('two forecasts of one quarter', 'uphold'), ('number misread', 'reject'), ('two forecasts of one quarter', 'uphold')])]\n  q4 rate: F8 13 ['F7', 'e3406', 'e5067', 'e4771', 'e5205'] I carry forward the sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance and allow a modest increase as Rubin supply expands and hyperscaler sales reaccelerate. Memory, power and long delivery lead times limit how much demand can ship this quarter. The estimate allows a small contribution from limited H200 sales if China authorizes purchases in time.\nR07 E claude SHA True {'v41': (3, None)} [['S1', 'F4: \"$35 billion\" is part of the longer expression \"$35 billion to $170 billion\" in e4857; copy it whole']]\n  readers: [(['yes'], []), (['no'], [])]\n  settle: [(['yes'], []), (['yes'], [])]\nR08 P claude SHA True {'v4': (2, [416.32, 416.32, None]), 'v8': (2, [384.8625, 384.8625, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes', 'yes'], []), (['yes', 'yes'], [\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\"])]\n  settle: [(['yes', 'yes'], [(\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", 'reject')]), (['yes', 'yes'], [(\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", 'uphold')])]\nR09 S claude SHA True {'v6': (4, [705.0, 735.0, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes'], []), (['no'], ['broken structure', 'wrong arithmetic'])]\n  settle: [(['yes'], [('broken structure', 'reject'), ('wrong arithmetic', 'reject')]), (['no'], [('broken structure', 'uphold'), ('wrong arithmetic', 'reject')])]\nR10 P codex SHA True {'v4': (2, [417.40000000000003, 417.40000000000003, None]), 'v8': (2, [386.4958, 386.4958, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes', 'yes'], []), (['yes', 'yes'], ['number misread'])]\n  settle: [(['yes', 'yes'], [('number misread', 'reject')]), (['yes', 'yes'], [('number misread', 'reject')])]\n  q4 rate: F9 12 ['F7', 'F8', 'e3406', 'e5067', 'e5205', 'e2652'] I carry the growth implied by Q3 guidance forward from my higher Q3 base. Expanding Rubin supply and recovering hyperscaler growth support that pace. Memory and infrastructure constraints limit further acceleration. I allow for a small contribution from H200 sales if authorization and deliveries arrive in time.\n  q4 rate: F15 93 ['F13', 'e3399', 'e3406', 'e5061', 'e5118'] I expect Data Center to take a slightly larger share as hyperscaler growth picks up and Rubin shipments expand. Any H200 sales recognized in the quarter are included in this segment share and in the company forecast.\nR11 O codex SHA True {'v29': (1, [16.0, 16.0, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes'], []), (['yes'], ['number misread'])]\n  settle: [(['yes'], [('number misread', 'reject')]), (['yes'], [('number misread', 'reject')])]\nR12 P-v8 codex SHA True {'v8': (2, [387.10125, 387.10125, None])} []\n  readers: [(['yes'], []), (['yes'], ['number misread', 'two forecasts of one quarter', \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\"])]\n  settle: [(['yes'], [('number misread', 'reject'), ('two forecasts of one quarter', 'reject'), (\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", 'reject')]), (['yes'], [('number misread', 'reject'), ('two forecasts of one quarter', 'reject'), (\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", 'uphold')])]\n  q4 rate: F12 13 ['F11', 'e3406', 'e2693', 'e5118', 'e5067', 'e5205', 'e2652'] I carry the guided Q3 sequential pace into Q4 with a small increase. Management expects hyperscale growth to pick up as Rubin supply expands. The estimate includes a small allowance for late H200 shipments if authorized. Memory availability and channel lead times limit the increase.\nfinal support Counter({('yes', 'yes'): 13, ('yes', 'no'): 1})\ndefect packet/type counts Counter({('number misread', ('reject',)): 6, (\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", ('reject', 'uphold')): 4, ('wrong arithmetic', ('reject',)): 2, ('two forecasts of one quarter', ('uphold',)): 1, ('broken structure', ('reject', 'uphold')): 1, ('two forecasts of one quarter', ('reject',)): 1})\ndefect individual claim counts Counter({('number misread', ('reject',)): 7, (\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", ('reject', 'uphold')): 4, ('wrong arithmetic', ('reject',)): 2, ('two forecasts of one quarter', ('uphold',)): 2, ('broken structure', ('reject', 'uphold')): 1, ('two forecasts of one quarter', ('reject',)): 1})\nE\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
