{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"head -n 14 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R06.md; head -n 18 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R06.md; rg -n '\"'^#|stop|D737|D738|blind'\"' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_*.py | head -n 60\"","aggregated_output":"# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\n\nYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown) and one answer to it,\nwritten in a JSON format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check accepted it. Read only what is given here.\n\nFor **every member** the answer forecasts, and for the answer as a whole, return JSON:\n\n{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n\nDefinitions:\n- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n\nTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\nbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n\nFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no): the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions.\nFor each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the passage words or the format rule that decides it. The\ndefect types are: number misread (a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes,\nallowing what the format rules permit); wrong arithmetic (a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs); part above or\nadded into its whole; two forecasts of one quarter (two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period);\nown estimate passed off as the market's reference (a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\nestimate or forecast); broken structure (a link that does not resolve, a figure depending on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an\noutcome not of the question's quantity, a rung whose band does not hold the outcome).\n\nReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n\n# The two readings\n1:\"\"\"The v13s blind conformance test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 with its [52] changes; D737, D738). $0 at the provider.\n3:  python3 blind_test.py build <out folder>      # the six registered requests, each in its own folder (system.txt, user.txt, schema.json)\n4:  python3 blind_test.py check <out folder>      # every writer's first answer through model.check_schema, from_answer and check\n5:  python3 blind_test.py packets <out folder>    # one reader packet per answer: the whole request text and the answer, checker hidden\n77:            return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', 'joint', draw, spec['calls'], family, [tv, dv], 'v13s')\n78:        return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', f'separate-{spec[\"member\"]}', draw, spec['calls'], pg.only(family, spec['member']), [spec['member']], 'v13s')\n82:    return pg.item(f'blind-{name}', 'single', draw, spec['calls'], family, [view_id], 'v13s')\n157:READER_BRIEF = \"\"\"# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R06.claude.json; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R06.codex.json; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R12.codex.json; rg -n '\\\"case\\\"|\\\"errors\\\"|\\\"decision\\\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Two reported quarters: FY2027 Q1 $81.62bn (e111) and Q2 $96.22bn (e4962). Q3 is $113.6bn (F6), taken from the outside estimate based on past guidance beats (e5056, with e5054/e5055 and the prior-quarter beat in e3200), against guidance of $108bn (e2652). Q4 is $128.368bn: the writer's own stated assumption of 13% sequential growth (F8), resting on guidance-implied growth of 12.24% (F7) and on e3406, where hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4. The sum is $419.808bn, which falls in rung 2 ($392bn to below $431.2bn). The consensus reference of $411bn (e5204) is also in rung 2. The rung is robust: using guidance as given (Q3 $108bn, Q4 at 12.24% growth, about $121.2bn) totals about $407bn, still rung 2. Rung 1 would need Q4 above about $139.8bn. Rung 3 would need Q4 below about $100.5bn.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F6\", \"F7\"], \"detail\": \"The request names NVIDIA revenue FY2027 Q3 as a shared quantity. Every figure the outcome uses for it must go through one forecast figure, and here that is F6 ($113.6bn). F7 (Q3 sequential growth, 12.24%) instead takes Q3 revenue directly from F4, the $108bn guidance. F8, the Q4 growth estimate, rests on F7, and F9/F10 build on F8. So the outcome's chain uses two different Q3 values: $108bn to set the growth rate, and $113.6bn as the base for Q4 and in the annual sum. The forecast's own Q3 would imply about 18.1% sequential growth, not 12.24%. The effect on the outcome is small (Q4 $128.4bn vs about $122.0bn on the guidance base), and the rung stays 2 either way.\"}]}\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v4\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"e111 and e4962 give $81.62 billion and $96.22 billion for Q1 and Q2. The answer adopts the $113.6 billion Q3 estimate from e5056, citing the prior guidance beat and Rubin shipments in e3200, e2693, and e5118. It assumes 13% sequential growth in Q4 based on the growth implied by e2652 and the outlook in e3406, tempered by the constraints in e5067, e4771, and e5205. That gives $128.368 billion for Q4 and $419.808 billion for the year, within rung 2.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": [\n    10\t    {\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\"],\n    13\t      \"detail\": \"e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F4 preserves the range in the quoted text but records the midpoint with no qualifier or bounds.\"\n    14\t    },\n    15\t    {\n    16\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    17\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F6\"],\n    18\t      \"detail\": \"The annual forecast uses $113.6 billion as Q3 revenue, while the Q4 growth assumption separately uses $108 billion as Q3 revenue through F7. Both Q3 values feed the annual forecast.\"\n    19\t    }\n    20\t  ]\n    21\t}     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v8\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue is $164.25 billion (e109, e2650). The stated assumptions of $112.5 billion in Q3 company revenue, 13% sequential growth in Q4, and a 93% second-half Data Center share yield $222.85 billion in second-half segment revenue. The resulting $387.10 billion falls in rung 2. The assumptions draw on e2652, e3200, e5056, e2693, e5118, e3406, e5061, e5223, e5067, and e5205.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": [\n    10\t    {\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F8\"],\n    13\t      \"detail\": \"e2652 gives $108.0 billion as the guidance midpoint with a plus-or-minus 2% range. F8 quotes that range but records no bounds and sets the qualifier to none.\"\n    14\t    },\n    15\t    {\n    16\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    17\t      \"figures\": [\"F8\", \"F10\", \"F11\", \"F12\", \"F13\"],\n    18\t      \"detail\": \"The Q4 forecast uses $108 billion as Q3 company revenue to calculate the sequential growth benchmark that informs F12, then uses $112.5 billion as Q3 company revenue when applying the chosen growth rate in F13.\"\n    19\t    },\n    20\t    {\n    21\t      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    22\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F5\", \"F6\"],\n    23\t      \"detail\": \"The $411 billion consensus in e5204 covers company revenue. F6 applies the reported Q2 Data Center share to the full year, making the $380.24 billion segment reference depend on the writer's assumption that the Q2 share holds for FY2027.\"\n    24\t    }\n    25\t  ]\n    26\t}3:  \"case\": \"P\",\n7:  \"errors\": [],\n54:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n57:  \"case\": \"P\",\n61:  \"errors\": [],\n124:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n127:  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n131:  \"errors\": [],\n206:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n209:  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n213:  \"errors\": [],\n261:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n264:  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n268:  \"errors\": [],\n317:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n320:  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n324:  \"errors\": [],\n385:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n388:  \"case\": \"S\",\n392:  \"errors\": [],\n438:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n441:  \"case\": \"S\",\n445:  \"errors\": [],\n479:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n482:  \"case\": \"O\",\n486:  \"errors\": [],\n519:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n522:  \"case\": \"O\",\n526:  \"errors\": [],\n564:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n567:  \"case\": \"E\",\n571:  \"errors\": [\n579:  \"decision\": \"refused\"\n582:  \"case\": \"E\",\n586:  \"errors\": [],\n623:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt | sed -n '36,46p;81,86p;100,103p;127,131p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/O/user.txt | sed -n '17,25p;42,46p'; rg -n '22|85|fragment' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"    36\t    Quote: Moody's has warned that spending on this scale is eating into free cash flow and pushing tech groups into heavier borrowing.\n    37\t- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    38\t    Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n    39\t- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    40\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    41\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e592, e3353, e3556, e3557, e3621, e3647, e3651, e3729, e4224, e5377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    42\t- e3142 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery 2026-08-27] AWS and NVIDIA announced plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and to bring Vera CPU-based infrastructure to AWS.\n    43\t    Quote: AWS and NVIDIA announced yesterday a major expansion of their 16-year collaboration, including plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and work to bring Vera CPU -based infrastructure to AWS.\n    44\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    45\t- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    46\t    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\n    81\t- e4807 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com, quoting Pablo Hernández de Cos in a BIS speech; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said on September 10 that AI investment was increasingly financed by debt, particularly private credit, and warned disappointing AI returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n    82\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n    83\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n    84\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n    85\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    86\t- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   100\t    Quote: OpenAI CEO Sam Altman backed the idea, saying OpenAI would also use independent evaluators and agreeing that the frontier needs to be paced.\n   101\t- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   102\t    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   103\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   127\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   129\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   130\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   131\t\n    17\t- e1970 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey) 2026-08-17; from gnews:127ca150cda794237261a87c 2026-08-17] SB Energy is reportedly targeting an IPO as early as September 2026 to raise at least $5 billion, with NVIDIA's second $1.5 billion investment tranche expected to be made at the IPO.\n    18\t    Quote: SB Energy is reportedly targeting an IPO as early as next month, seeking to raise at least $5 billion — and Nvidia’s second $1.5 billion tranche is expected to land there, putting Nvidia, OpenAI, and SoftBank all on the cap table of a newly public AI power company.\n    19\t- e1989 [fact; CNBC; original source SB Energy, SoftBank, and NVIDIA ; from gnews:a95b9986d8697704ffa17843 2026-08-17] For the Ohio project, SB Energy and SoftBank plan power sources supporting 10 GW and at least $4.2 billion of regional grid investment; NVIDIA will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    20\t    Quote: As part of the Ohio deal, SB Energy and SoftBank will build power sources supporting 10 gigawatts of energy and invest at least $4.2 billion into regional grid infrastructure. Nvidia will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1984, e1999, e2022, e2045; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e2080 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:a8855077e115780d8a3e8e06 2026-08-18] Bank of America estimated that NVIDIA had committed approximately $300 billion to ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investments and roughly $230 billion in residual-value guarantees or backstop commitments.\n    23\t    Quote: Bank of America estimates that NVIDIA has committed approximately $300 billion in capital to its ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investments and roughly $230 billion in residual value guarantees or backstop commitments.\n    24\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2095, e2170; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    25\t- e2207 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA and Poolside, as reported by Newcomer based on a Poolside investor letter 2026-08-20; from gnews:1e66644cc2e64e6295fd657a 2026-08-21] NVIDIA’s Poolside transaction includes a $6 billion non-exclusive Model Factory license, a $1 billion investment at a $12 billion pre-money valuation, and the transfer of 109 employees to NVIDIA.\n    42\t    Quote: The firm estimates roughly $300 billion of committed capital to date, including about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n    43\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2095; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    44\t- e2889 [party_statement; Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:aec1fae1b903de5c43ba7fdf 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said on Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year, in addition to $47.9 billion held in private companies.\n    45\t    Quote: The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.\n    46\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2960; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n170:| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 (the fiscal year-end balance the annual report publishes) | 25/50/75/100 | control |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'proxy|total capital|not data|e5014|\\\"id\\\"|rung|\\\"basis_text\\\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json | cut -c 1-550; rg -n 'E|P-v4|adjudic' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/{BUILD.txt,codex-writers.log} | cut -c 1-300; sed -n '178,244p' src/hivemind/model.py; ls -a | rg 'venv|python'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'proxy|total capital|not data|e5014|\\\"id\\\"|rung|\\\"basis_text\\\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json | cut -c 1-550; rg -n 'E|P-v4|adjudic' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/{BUILD.txt,codex-writers.log} | cut -c 1-300; sed -n '178,244p' src/hivemind/model.py; ls -a | rg 'venv|python'\"","aggregated_output":"4:      \"id\": \"F1\",\n22:      \"basis_text\": null,\n28:      \"id\": \"F2\",\n46:      \"basis_text\": null,\n52:      \"id\": \"F3\",\n65:      \"evidence_id\": \"e5014\",\n70:      \"basis_text\": null,\n76:      \"id\": \"F4\",\n94:      \"basis_text\": null,\n100:      \"id\": \"F5\",\n118:      \"basis_text\": null,\n124:      \"id\": \"F6\",\n142:      \"basis_text\": null,\n148:      \"id\": \"F7\",\n166:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate a modest deduction from company-wide plans, mostly for capital spending outside data centers, including Amazon's retail operations, with a smaller allowance for project delays. The four companies' AI infrastructure indication and the proximity of Meta's AI spending estimate to its total guidance support treating data centers as the dominant use of capital. The delay tally covers a broader pool of projects and their full project values, so I allow only for the portion likely to displace spending by these compa\n172:      \"id\": \"F8\",\n190:      \"basis_text\": null,\n200:      \"rung\": 4,\n202:      \"reasons\": \"The four companies' indicated AI infrastructure spending above $730 billion (F1) gives a rung 4 reference for the narrower data-center question. The $745 billion company-wide tracker (F2) sits at the top of Zacks' range (F3) and below the higher outside estimate (F4). Alphabet's raised guidance, Amazon's memory-cost-driven revision, and strong reported cloud demand support using that company-wide base. I deduct $30 billion (F7) for spending outside data centers and for some projects slipping into later years. The AWS GPU e\n204:      \"cites\": [\"e1947\", \"e1856\", \"e5014\", \"e2458\", \"e1254\", \"e1649\", \"e1549\", \"e3686\", \"e4404\", \"e4405\", \"e4406\", \"e122\", \"e5292\", \"e5150\", \"e2759\", \"e5376\"],\n208:        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5014\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/codex-writers.log:2:E codex exit 0\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/codex-writers.log:4:P-v4 codex exit 0\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/BUILD.txt:2:P-v4  separate-v4   members ['v4'] passages 296 (79 quotes absent from the gate) request 169937 chars\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/BUILD.txt:6:E     single        members ['v41'] passages 15 (0 quotes absent from the gate) request 26002 chars\n    failure = TransportError(last or 'no answer')\n    if last_http:\n        failure.http_status, failure.response_body, failure.response_body_base64 = last_http\n    raise failure\n\n\ndef check_schema(value, schema, path='$', root=None):\n    \"\"\"A small validator for the subset of JSON Schema the prompts use: object/required/additionalProperties,\n    array/items/minItems/maxItems, string/enum/pattern, integer, number, boolean, null in anyOf, and $ref into the root's $defs.\"\"\"\n    root = root if root is not None else schema\n    if '$ref' in schema:\n        name = schema['$ref'].rsplit('/', 1)[1]\n        schema = root['$defs'][name]\n    kind = schema.get('type')\n    if 'anyOf' in schema:\n        # a value matching no option reports the closest options' errors (fewest), not merely the last option's\n        failures = []\n        for option in schema['anyOf']:\n            errors = check_schema(value, option, path, root)\n            if not errors:\n                return []\n            failures.append(errors)\n        fewest = min((len(errors) for errors in failures), default=0)\n        closest = list(dict.fromkeys(error for errors in failures if len(errors) == fewest for error in errors))\n        return closest or [f'{path}: matches no option']\n    if kind == 'object':\n        if not isinstance(value, dict):\n            return [f'{path}: expected object']\n        errors = []\n        for name in schema.get('required', []):\n            if name not in value:\n                errors.append(f'{path}.{name}: required')\n        properties = schema.get('properties', {})\n        for name, item in value.items():\n            if name in properties:\n                errors.extend(check_schema(item, properties[name], f'{path}.{name}', root))\n            elif schema.get('additionalProperties') is False:\n                errors.append(f'{path}.{name}: not allowed')\n        return errors\n    if kind == 'array':\n        if not isinstance(value, list):\n            return [f'{path}: expected array']\n        errors = []\n        if len(value) < schema.get('minItems', 0):\n            errors.append(f'{path}: fewer than {schema[\"minItems\"]} items')\n        if 'maxItems' in schema and len(value) > schema['maxItems']:\n            errors.append(f'{path}: more than {schema[\"maxItems\"]} items')\n        for index, item in enumerate(value):\n            errors.extend(check_schema(item, schema.get('items', {}), f'{path}[{index}]', root))\n        return errors\n    if kind == 'string':\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            return [f'{path}: expected string']\n        if 'enum' in schema and value not in schema['enum']:\n            return [f'{path}: {value!r} not in {schema[\"enum\"]}']\n        if 'pattern' in schema and not re.search(schema['pattern'], value):\n            return [f'{path}: {value!r} does not match {schema[\"pattern\"]}']\n        return []\n    if kind == 'integer':\n        return [] if isinstance(value, int) and not isinstance(value, bool) else [f'{path}: expected integer']\n    if kind == 'number':\n        return [] if isinstance(value, (int, float)) and not isinstance(value, bool) else [f'{path}: expected number']\n    if kind == 'boolean':\n        return [] if isinstance(value, bool) else [f'{path}: expected boolean']\n    if kind == 'null':\n        return [] if value is None else [f'{path}: expected null']\n    return []\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
