n   271\t   \"number misread\": [\n   272\t    \"claude:reject\",\n   273\t    \"codex:reject\"\n   274\t   ],\n   275\t   \"two forecasts of one quarter\": [\n   276\t    \"claude:reject\",\n   277\t    \"codex:reject\"\n   278\t   ],\n   279\t   \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\": [\n   280\t    \"claude:reject\",\n   281\t    \"codex:uphold\"\n   282\t   ]\n   283\t  }\n   284\t ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R11.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"The e1919 quote uses \\\"up to $3 billion\\\" for the entire deal and describes the second tranche as \\\"an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular\\\" conditions. The format takes the qualifier from the quote immediately before the number, with exceptions for \\\"or more\\\" and \\\"or less\\\" immediately after it. The quote gives the $1 billion tranche no upper-limit qualifier. F9 records the amount and identifies the tranche as contingent.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R12.codex.json:28:      \"why\": \"The reference rule allows computed figures but says the reference and the figures beneath it are \\\"never an estimate or a forecast.\\\" The $411 billion in e5204 is consensus for total company revenue, and F5 measures the segment's share in Q2. Applying that share to the full year requires the writer's own assumption about the FY2027 revenue mix, so F6's $380.24 billion is the writer's segment estimate.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R09.codex.json:18:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R02.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"The e1919 quote says “up to $3 billion” for the full deal and “an additional $1 billion” for the milestone tranche. The format takes the numeral and its qualifier from the quote. The unsupported Q3 timing does not change either.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R02.codex.json:21:      \"why\": \"The e2080 quote says “about $70 billion in equity investments.” F7 preserves the amount, scale, and qualifier. The quote does not establish the Q3 timing or the paid-and-unpaid interpretation, but those claims do not change the quoted number.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R02.codex.json:25:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R02.codex.json:28:      \"why\": \"The format says “The value is the number as written” and records the source qualifier separately. F8 correctly stores 42 with “more than.” The sum of the two recorded point values is 42 + 25 = 67. In prose, the underlying total is more than $67 billion.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R02.claude.json | sed -n '25,56p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R12.codex.json | sed -n '15,29p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R09.codex.json | sed -n '2,23p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R02.claude.json | sed -n '25,56p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R12.codex.json | sed -n '15,29p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R09.codex.json | sed -n '2,23p'\"","aggregated_output":"    25\t  },\n    26\t  {\n    27\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    28\t   \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n    29\t   \"figures\": [\n    30\t    \"F8\",\n    31\t    \"F1\",\n    32\t    \"F9\"\n    33\t   ],\n    34\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    35\t   \"why\": \"42 + 25 = 67; qualifier on F8 does not make the sum wrong\"\n    36\t  },\n    37\t  {\n    38\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    39\t   \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    40\t   \"figures\": [\n    41\t    \"F1\",\n    42\t    \"F2\",\n    43\t    \"F3\"\n    44\t   ],\n    45\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    46\t   \"why\": \"F3 = difference of a reported and a guidance source; nothing beneath is an estimate or forecast\"\n    47\t  }\n    48\t ],\n    49\t \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the Claude adjudicator hand-back (decisions and figures verbatim; reasons shortened).\"\n    50\t}    15\t    },\n    16\t    {\n    17\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    18\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    19\t      \"figures\": [\"F8\", \"F10\", \"F11\", \"F12\", \"F13\"],\n    20\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    21\t      \"why\": \"The rule requires \\\"one forecast figure for each quantity and period\\\" and allows \\\"outside figures beneath it.\\\" F8 is NVIDIA's guidance, which F11 uses to calculate a benchmark for choosing the Q4 growth rate. F10 is the sole Q3 company revenue forecast and supplies the revenue base for F13.\"\n    22\t    },\n    23\t    {\n    24\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    25\t      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    26\t      \"figures\": [\"F4\", \"F5\", \"F6\"],\n    27\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    28\t      \"why\": \"The reference rule allows computed figures but says the reference and the figures beneath it are \\\"never an estimate or a forecast.\\\" The $411 billion in e5204 is consensus for total company revenue, and F5 measures the segment's share in Q2. Applying that share to the full year requires the writer's own assumption about the FY2027 revenue mix, so F6's $380.24 billion is the writer's segment estimate.\"\n    29\t    }\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"The calculation gives a $720 billion midpoint for total capital spending. The answer acknowledges that some of that spending, such as Amazon's logistics spending, falls outside data centers. It gives no estimate or bound for that share. More than $20 billion outside data centers would put the forecast below rung 4, even if all four companies meet their total spending projections.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F5\", \"F9\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"e5014 says the four companies will spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion 'on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.' F5 labels that company-wide total as data-center capex. F9 also sums company-wide spending without an adjustment, although the format rule says the outcome's 'what is the member's outcome key,' which specifies data-center capex.\"\n    15\t    },\n    16\t    {\n    17\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    18\t      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n    19\t      \"figures\": [\"F9\"],\n    20\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    21\t      \"why\": \"F1, F2, F3 and F8 sum to $705–735 billion, whose midpoint is $720 billion. The operation rule explicitly says 'you may declare the midpoint as value,' so F9 can report $720 billion with sum as its declared operation.\"\n    22\t    }\n    23\t  ]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
