{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"agent_message","text":"**STOP is correct as registered.** The checker refused a correct $35B figure, which alone stops the test. R06 also triggered the registered reader stop, but R12 exposes an inconsistency in how that defect was judged. I recommend **(i)**: fix the checker, get Alex’s rulings, and run the $0 blind test once before requesting the paid re-send. [Small design, §5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:244)\n\n## Findings, ranked by severity\n\n1. **High — S1 refused a correct answer.** The source gives $35B in 2026 and $170B in 2030. The checker grabs the whole `X to Y` span before it looks for individual amounts. The narrow fix is to split that span during quote scanning when the sentence explicitly describes a change between two times. Keep genuine ranges whole, including “guidance ranges from $195B to $205B.” In a read-only replay of all 12 answers, a trial split changed only R07, which published at $34B, rung 3. The quote check accepted each endpoint separately and rejected the stitched $35B–$170B range. [Source quote](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:30) · [Range scanner](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:32) · [Recorded refusal](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:567)\n\n2. **High — R06 and R12 use the same forecasting method.** Both derive a roughly 12.25% growth benchmark from $108B Q3 guidance. Both choose 13% for Q4 and apply it to their own higher Q3 forecast: $113.6B in R06 and $112.5B in R12. R12 then converts company revenue to Data Center revenue. In both answers, guidance informs the growth assumption and only one Q3 dollar forecast enters the revenue calculation. Both adjudicators upheld R06 and rejected R12 on reasoning that applies equally to both. The reported ~$6B difference is a sensitivity: swapping R06’s Q3 base to $108B while holding 13% fixed changes Q4 by $6.3B and leaves the rung unchanged. **Have Alex settle the reader rule.** I recommend allowing a clearly identified outside growth benchmark when one Q3 forecast supplies the actual revenue calculation. D738 already puts the interpretation of an estimate’s assumptions with readers. [R06 answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/P-v4/answers/codex.json:118) · [R12 answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/P-v8/answers/codex.json:194) · [R06 adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R06.codex.json:10) · [R12 adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R12.codex.json:16) · [D738](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11265)\n\n3. **Medium — The counts reconcile, but the report overstates three conclusions.** I reproduced 12 schema passes, 11 published replies, one S1 refusal, and every figure and rung in the table. The records contain 24 initial reviews, 18 adjudications across nine replies, and two refusal reviews. After adjudication where needed, 13 of 14 forecasts had support from both families, including the forecast in the refused reply. R09 was the sole support split, and three replies needed no adjudication. All six cases had a publishable reply supported by both families, and both joint replies had both forecasts supported. Both adjudicators rejected all seven number-misread claims and both arithmetic claims. The report gives the midpoint explanation for both arithmetic claims, but R02’s was about `42 + 25 = 67` with a qualifier. It also calls the $18B in R02 “scheduled payments,” while the source states commitments for the rest of the year. Finally, “no refusal on an unstated rule” should read **one disputed**: both reviewers agreed the amount was right, and they split on whether the request stated the condition. The historical 0 of 90 figure links also checks out across the first attempts and retries. [Blind report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md:29) · [Tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:17) · [R02 arithmetic review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R02.codex.json:23) · [Commitments quote](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/O/user.txt:44) · [Historical count](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md:33)\n\n## Two questions for Alex\n\nBoth splits go to Alex under the council rule for reader disagreements. The second question is about what evidence can support the narrower forecast. D737 already requires the published figure to answer the quantity asked. [Council rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/.claude/skills/council-loop/SKILL.md:163) · [D737](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11245)\n\n1. **When can we call a number the market’s estimate?** Analysts expect $411B in company revenue, and Data Center was 92.5% of the latest quarter. Can we call the resulting ~$380B the market’s full-year Data Center estimate? **A.** Allow it if the calculation and its inputs are shown, as the earlier design does. **B.** Call it our comparison estimate unless an outside source supplies the full-year share or the full-year segment figure. I recommend **B**. Morningstar’s direct $361B estimate is a clear comparison case. The same rule would ask for evidence that the $18B of commitments is included in the $25B balance and will be paid by year-end before attributing the $7B remainder to an outside source. [Earlier design](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:75) · [Reader disagreement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R12.codex.json:24) · [Direct estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/P/user.txt:591)\n\n2. **How much evidence do we need to turn total capex into a data-center forecast?** R09 used a $720B midpoint for total spending and acknowledged spending outside data centers. More than $20B of exclusions would move that midpoint below the $700B cutoff. **A.** Allow the total as a disclosed stand-in when the sources say most spending goes toward AI infrastructure. **B.** Require a stated deduction or a supported bound that shows where data-center spending lands. I recommend **B**. R04 shows the difference by making a $30B deduction explicit. The two articles the Claude adjudicator compared describe **five** companies, while R09 answers for **four**. [Four-company source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:83) · [Five-company comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:128) · [R04 deduction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json:148)\n\n**One next test.** After the fix and rulings, use the stored examples to verify that both reader families apply the same rule to R06 and R12 and can distinguish the boundary examples above. Then run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers. Stop on any §5 failure, including a correct answer refused, an unstated refusal condition, a confirmed defect in an accepted answer, incomplete readings, a case with zero usable replies, or a joint case without one reply supporting both forecasts. A clean result is the basis for asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send. This review made no file changes. [Registered stop conditions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:250)"}}
